# Database access

Controllers and the UI never talk to a repository. They go through `ClinicService` (clinic data) or `UserService` (users).

## Three implementations, one active

The service layer depends only on interfaces in `repository/` (`OwnerRepository`, `PetRepository`, …). Spring loads **one** package from the profile:

| Profile | Package | Style |
|---|---|---|
| `spring-data-jpa` (default) | `repository.springdatajpa` | Spring Data + `@Query` JPQL |
| `jpa` | `repository.jpa` | Hand-written `EntityManager` |
| `jdbc` | `repository.jdbc` | `JdbcTemplate` + row mappers |

If you add or change a method on the interface, update **all three** implementations. Default local profile is `hsqldb,spring-data-jpa`.

## Spring Data conventions

- Method names follow Spring Data query derivation where that is enough.
- Use `@Query` with `join fetch` when the caller needs children after the transaction ends (`spring.jpa.open-in-view=false`). Owners load pets this way.
- Deletes that Spring Data cannot derive go on `*Override` + `*Impl` in `springdatajpa/`.

## Lookups

Missing rows: repositories may throw; `ClinicServiceImpl` catches that and returns `null`. Do not compare JPA entities with `equals` across sessions — compare **ids** (nested pet GET/PUT already does this).
